Sprite option block.

A) - Definition

The options block is a list of option items where each option item is structured
as follows:


popt_siz  $00	long	size of option
popt_nam  $04	long	name of option
popt_dta  $08		data for this option
   ....    as much memory as the option needs
	dc.w	0	make sure that this is on word boundary


The list must be terminated by a long word of 0.
If there are several option items in the list, they are put one at the end
of the other.


As an example, a sprite option block could be as follows (example code
provided by P Witte):


sp_option
	 dc.l	 sp_option2-*	  Size of this option
	 dc.l	 'TEST' 	  Option type. It's Per's option!
	 [... whatever the option needs ...]
	 ds.w	 0		  Must be word aligned
sp_option2
	 dc.l	 sp_option_end-*  Size of this option
	 dc.l	 'HINT' 	  Option type
	 dc.w	 12
	 dc.b	 'Hello World!'
	 ds.w	 0		  Must be word aligned
sp_option_end
	 dc.l	 0		  No other option


The length of the sprite must be indicated in such a way such that it can be
added to a register ax that points to the start of an option, to then point to
the start of the next option (or the long word 0 which signals the end of the
option block):

add.l	(ax)+,ax

where ax is any address register.



By design, there is no requirement that an option needs to be limlted
in size.
			

Moreover, the type of sprite option is not pre-determined. Sprite designers
thus can devise any type of sprite option they desire.


At the time of writing, there is one known type of option block:

	"HINT"

which, as data (at popt_dta) takes a word for the length of a string, followed
by the bytes of the string.


B - Modification
The main charcateristic of an option item is that it need not be static, it
may be modified by a suitable configuration program.

In the aboove example, the string of the HINT option item could be changed to
any other string (within the limits of SMSQDOSO strings).

This means that
	    
a) each option item will most probably need its own configuration program.
				    
At the time of writing, there is no published program that will allow
for editing of options in a sprite.

				
b) the length of an option block might change if the length of an option item
is changed.
This would have to be taken into account by the designer/programmer of the
configuration program.	It is strongly suggested that, when this is the case,
the newly enlarged item is moved to the end of the option block, all other items
being moved up to take up the slack space.


c) a programmer can not rely on an option item being at a specific offset
from the start of the option block, since a configuaation program may have
modified a preceeding item. It is necessary to iterate over the option
items until on gets to the correct one.



